I tried several alternatives such as:
1. Let each point find its closest one, then merge the lines (fully parallel) → [3763d6d](https://github.com/cupy/cupy/pull/7875/commits/3763d6da5e4e239c5c36b4948c02ddfb89d1a154)
2. Let each point find its closest one in a window in a single kernel (fully parallel) → https://github.com/cupy/cupy/pull/7875/commits/503acc36bde01f908e91993dcc671ddac33a2892
3. For each row, let each point find its closest line from the previous row (SciPy behaviour, data dependency between row results, dynamic size of lines). → [1f9147c](https://github.com/cupy/cupy/pull/7875/commits/1f9147c59b5ac960b571049201cd21f4040511be)

While 1 and 2 are parallel, they do not match the SciPy behaviour 100% and are invalid with respect the reference paper upon SciPy is based on, which causes some mismatches in some tests, and therefore potentially not complying with the `find_peaks_cwt` signature. 3 can parallelize the per-row matching, at the expense of having to recompute the current valid lines on each iteration, since new ones can appear dynamically, which cost more than .

